.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::Pidfile 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::Pidfile
.Nd locked PID file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::Pidfile;

my $pidfile = FuguLib::Pidfile->new(path => '/var/run/mydaemon.pid');

if (my $pid = $pidfile->is_running) {
    die "already running as pid $pid\en";
}

$pidfile->acquire;
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages one PID file.
It also reports if the process that the file names still runs.
The module exists so that a daemon,
its
.Xr rc.d 8
script,
and a control utility can all agree on the same question.
.Pp
Every write takes the lock before it truncates.
Thus a concurrent reader never sees an empty file.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates an object for the named file.
The method does not create and does not read the file at this point.
.Pp
This is the argument:
.Bl -tag -width "pathXX"
.It Fa path
The PID file.
This argument is necessary.
.El
.Ss path
.Fn path
returns the file that the object manages.
.Ss write_pid
.Fn write_pid "$pid"
writes
.Fa $pid
and a newline to the PID file,
then releases the lock.
The default for
.Fa $pid
is the process ID of the caller.
.Ss acquire
.Fn acquire "$pid"
writes the PID and keeps the locked handle open.
The lock lives until
.Fn release ,
or until the process exits.
While one process holds the lock, an
.Fn acquire
from a second process fails at once and does not wait.
Thus
.Dq am I already running
has an authoritative answer that no
.Fn read_pid
race can spoil.
.Ss release
.Fn release
drops a lock that
.Fn acquire
holds.
The file itself stays.
.Ss read_pid
.Fn read_pid
returns the process ID that the file holds.
The first line must be a sequence of decimal digits.
If the contents are different, or if the file is absent, the method
returns no PID.
.Ss remove
.Fn remove
removes the PID file.
.Ss is_running
.Fn is_running
returns the process ID from the file if that process is alive.
The method uses
.Xr FuguLib::Process 3p
for the liveness check.
.Ss is_stale
.Fn is_stale
reports if the PID file names a process that is not alive now.
In this condition, a daemon can take the file and does not refuse to
start.
.Ss error
.Fn error
returns the most recent failure as a message that a log can carry.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn new
returns an object.
.Pp
.Fn write_pid ,
.Fn acquire
and
.Fn remove
return 1 on success.
They return
.Dv undef
on failure and put the reason in
.Fn error .
.Pp
.Fn release
returns 1.
.Pp
.Fn read_pid
and
.Fn is_running
return a process ID,
or
.Dv undef
when there is no process ID to report.
.Pp
.Fn is_stale
returns 0 when the file holds no readable PID.
Thus an absent PID file is not stale.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example prevents a second start and holds the file for the life of
the daemon:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $pidfile = FuguLib::Pidfile->new(path => '/var/run/mydaemon.pid');

if (my $pid = $pidfile->is_running) {
    die "mydaemon already running as pid $pid\en";
}

FuguLib::Daemon->daemonize(
    pidfile => '/var/run/mydaemon.pid',
);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies when
.Fa path
is absent or empty.
That is a programming error.
.Pp
No other method dies.
They report a failure to open, lock, write or unlink the file through
the return value and
.Fn error .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr flock 2 ,
.Xr unlink 2 ,
.Xr FuguLib::Daemon 3p ,
.Xr FuguLib::Process 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn write_pid
releases its lock when it closes the file,
at the end of the call.
The lock puts two concurrent writes in sequence.
It does not hold the PID file for the life of the daemon.
Use
.Fn acquire
for that.
.Fn read_pid
does not take a lock.
.Pp
A check with
.Fn is_running
and then an action on the answer is a race condition.
In between, the process can exit,
or a different process can claim the file.
.Fn acquire
has no such window.
.Pp
.Fn is_running
has the same limits as the liveness check of
.Xr FuguLib::Process 3p .
That check cannot show the difference between the initial process and a
later process with the same ID.
